# Source and scope audit

The exact target is Danny Calegari's Question 13.2 in *Problems in foliations and laminations of 3-manifolds*, version 0.78 (2002), printed p. 29, [arXiv:math/0209081](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0209081). That page was read in full and inspected visually. The question inherits the minimal, taut, $C^2$, atoroidal hypotheses and nonzero Godbillon–Vey evaluation from Question 13.1. A global defining form is assumed, so the foliation is cooriented. The displayed convention is $d\alpha=\alpha\wedge\omega$.

The ordinary evaluation on the fundamental class is interpreted in the closed oriented category. The candidate explicitly asserts no conclusion for noncompact or boundary variants. The source's definition of tautness is the existence of a transverse circle meeting every leaf (Definition 1.1, p. 1). Minimality rules out spherical leaves in the case at issue.

Question 13.1 asks for existence of a gauge with a weak sign condition on the Godbillon–Vey density. Question 13.2 instead assumes a contact form and asks for tightness. They are distinct targets: no existence assertion follows from the conditional proof.

## Literature and imported statements

The published paper of Vogel (2011), printed p. 42, explicitly attributes to Eliashberg–Thurston the fact that every contact structure sufficiently $C^0$ close to a taut foliation is symplectically fillable and tight. This is the neighborhood statement needed here. It is stronger than the bare assertion that at least one tight approximation exists. The statement and adjoining definitions were checked visually.

Dathe–Rukimbira's 2008 author preprint, Proposition 3.3, repeats the required tightness statement and locates it on p. 50 of *Confoliations*. Their Proposition 3.4 gives a closely related deformation argument when the defining form is closed. That proposition is prior related work, not a direct proof of the present nonclosed-defining-form assertion.

Vogel (2016), Definition 2.2, explicitly allows $C^1$ contact plane fields. Theorem 2.9 states Gray stability for a smooth family of smooth contact structures. The candidate respects that regularity: it regularizes forms on a finite compact parameter interval before using Gray. It also proves a boundary-preserving smoothing lemma to rule out an overtwisted disk for a merely $C^1$ form.

The complete PDFs of these primary papers were available and their relevant sections were read. The entire Eliashberg–Thurston book was not independently retrieved. Its theorem is imported using the explicit primary-paper formulations above; the symplectic filling theorem is not reconstructed in this attempt.

## Bounded status and priority check

On 2026-09-30, primary-source searches combining Calegari Question 13.2, Godbillon–Vey, contact forms, tightness, and linear deformations located the original question, the classical approximation literature, and the related Dathe–Rukimbira result. No direct published resolution of this exact question was located in this bounded search. This is not evidence of first discovery. The proof is a short deduction from established contact topology, and its historical priority is left unconfirmed.

The exact pinned record, prior report, current queue, all-ref target-path history, assessment history, state, related-target groups, remote target branches, and all-state pull-request search were checked before this attempt. No prior campaign attempt or additional exact duplicate was found. No source correction was silently substituted for the problem. Bibliographic URLs and PDF hashes are recorded in [source_manifest.json](source_manifest.json).
